# Creating Libraries in C

Overview:
---------
Libraries allow you to reuse frequently used functions and achieve modularity. You can create your own static or dynamic libraries.

---

## 1. Static Library
-----------------
Definition:
- Static libraries are collections of object files.
- Conventionally end with `.a` extension (archive files).
- Created using the `ar` command.
- Supports static linking: library functions are copied into executable at compile time.
- Advantages:
  - No runtime dependencies
  - Faster execution (no dynamic symbol resolution)
  - Portable
- Disadvantages:
  - Any library update requires recompiling the main program
  - Larger executable size

Example: User-Defined Functions

sum.c:
int sum(int i, int j) {
    return i + j;
}

mul.c:
int mul(int i, int j) {
    return i * j;
}

print.c:
#include <stdio.h>
void print(char *p) {
    printf("%s\n", p);
}

Steps:

1. Compile source files into object files:
$ gcc -c sum.c -o sum.o
$ gcc -c mul.c -o mul.o
$ gcc -c print.c -o print.o

2. Create static library using ar:
$ ar -rcs libabc.a sum.o mul.o print.o
Options:
- r: Replace/add objects
- c: Create library if new
- s: Create symbol table

3. List contents:
$ ar -t libabc.a
Output:
sum.o
mul.o
print.o

4. Use library in a program:

statp.c:
#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
    int r;
    print("Hello");
    r = sum(10, 20);
    printf("r = %d\n", r);
    r = mul(10, 20);
    printf("r = %d\n", r);
    return 0;
}

5. Compile and link:
$ gcc statp.c libabc.a -o statp
$ gcc -static statp.c libabc.a -o statp   # Fully static

Check:
$ file statp
# Output: ELF 64-bit LSB executable ... statically linked

