## 2. Dynamic Library (Shared Object)
---------------------------------
Definition:
- Collection of position-independent object files.
- Extension: `.so` (Linux), `.dll` (Windows).
- Supports dynamic linking: functions not copied into executable at compile time.
- Advantages:
  - Shared by multiple programs
  - Smaller executable size
  - Easy to update
- Disadvantages:
  - Runtime dependency
  - Slightly slower than static linking
  - Potential version mismatch or unauthorized sharing

Steps:

1. Compile source files with -fPIC (Position Independent Code):
$ gcc -c -fPIC sum.c
$ gcc -c -fPIC mul.c
$ gcc -c -fPIC print.c

2. Create shared library:
$ gcc -shared -o libxyz.so sum.o mul.o print.o
$ file libxyz.so
# Output: ELF 64-bit LSB shared object ... dynamically linked

3. Program using shared library:

dynap.c:
#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
    int r;
    print("Hello from dynamic library!");
    r = sum(5, 15);
    printf("Sum = %d\n", r);
    r = mul(3, 6);
    printf("Mul = %d\n", r);
    return 0;
}

4. Compile:
$ gcc dynap.c ./libxyz.so -o dynap
$ file dynap
# Output: dynamically linked

5. Check dependencies:
$ ldd dynap
# Output:
# linux-vdso.so.1 => (0x00007ffdcadf9000)
# ./libxyz.so => ./libxyz.so (0x00007f04d00c5000)
# libc.so.6 => /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libc.so.6
# /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2

