## 3. Linking Concepts
---------------------
- Linking: Combining object code with libraries to produce an executable.
- Types:
  1. Static Linking: Address known at compile time
  2. Load-Time Linking: Address resolved when program loads
  3. Run-Time Linking: Function loaded on demand at runtime

---

## 4. Dynamic Run-Time Linking (dlopen API)
-------------------------------------------
- Also called dynamic linking with dynamic loading
- Useful for plugins and JIT compilation
- Load and unload functions on demand
- Requires <dlfcn.h> API

Important Functions:
- dlopen(): Load library
- dlsym(): Get symbol address
- dlerror(): Error message
- dlclose(): Unload library

Example: dynamic_load.c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <dlfcn.h>

int main() {
    int r, op, a, b;
    void *handler;
    int (*p)(int,int);

    printf("Enter two numbers: ");
    scanf("%d%d",&a,&b);

    printf("Enter your option:\n1: sum\n2: mul\n");
    scanf("%d",&op);

    handler = dlopen("./libxyz.so", RTLD_LAZY);
    if(!handler) { printf("%s\n", dlerror()); return 1; }

    switch(op) {
        case 1: p = dlsym(handler, "sum"); break;
        case 2: p = dlsym(handler, "mul"); break;
        default: printf("Invalid option\n"); dlclose(handler); return 1;
    }

    if(!p) { printf("%s\n", dlerror()); dlclose(handler); return 1; }

    r = (*p)(a,b);
    printf("Result = %d\n", r);

    dlclose(handler);
    return 0;
}

Compile:
$ gcc dynamic_load.c -ldl -o dynamic_load

Check with nm: symbols for sum and mul are not present (loaded at runtime)

